      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Quadratic ReDoS in NAMED_RUN: adjacent \\s+ quantifiers around the optional connector backtrack O(n^2) on a long whitespace run.\n\nextractPlaceReferences runs the module-level NAMED_RUN regex over up to 200,000 chars of untrusted prose (a friend's email, an emailed set of directions). The pattern's optional connector `(?:\\s+(?:de|du|...|y|et|and)\\s+)?` is immediately followed by `(?:\\s+\\p{Lu}...)+`, so the trailing `\\s+` of the connector and the leading `\\s+` of the repeated group compete for the same whitespace run; when no capitalised token follows, the engine tries every split (N-1, N-2, ... 1) and for each split walks the remainder, roughly N^2/2 steps.\n\nImpact: CPU exhaustion / hang of the process that calls extractPlaceReferences; in a long-running integration this is a per-request denial of service. No confidentiality or integrity impact.\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker sends the victim a text document (the use case the command advertises: \"an emailed set of directions\") that contains `Ab y` followed by ~199,990 space characters and then a lowercase letter, well under the 200,000-char and 2 MB caps. The victim runs `wayfinding-audit refs <file>` or the /place-references command. At position 0 the regex matches `Ab`, enters the connector with `y`, then backtracks across every possible split of the whitespace run between the connector's trailing `\\s+` and the group's leading `\\s+`, on the order of 2e10 steps, pinning a CPU core for tens of seconds to minutes before returning no match. Any service that wraps the library (n8n, MCP) hangs for the same duration per request. Timing was not measured here (no execution); the estimate follows from the backtracking structure.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker supplies the prose document passed to extractPlaceReferences (the documented input is third-party text such as an email); Input must contain a capitalised token, a lowercase connector word, then a long whitespace run not followed by a capitalised token; total length under 200,000 chars.\n\nRecommendation: Make the whitespace between tokens unambiguous: drop the trailing `\\s+` from the connector so the group's `\\s+` is the only whitespace consumer (e.g. `(?:\\s+(?:de|du|...|and))?(?:\\s+\\p{Lu}[...]+)+`), or tokenise on whitespace first and detect runs over the token list instead of a regex. Add a timing test with the `Ab y` + long-whitespace shape to test-security.ts, and cap consecutive whitespace (e.g. collapse `\\s{2,}` to one space) before scanning.\n\nCWE-1333. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
