        {
          "ruleId": "uncategorized",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Step text from untrusted directions is emitted without sanitize(), so control/ANSI escape sequences pass through to the terminal and downstream prompts.\n\nsanitize() strips \\x00-\\x1f, \\x7f and bracket characters from landmarks, anchor label and start_facing, but the step text taken from stdin (the bulk of the untrusted input) is only .strip()ped and appended verbatim at lines 118 and 141; main() prints it to stdout at line 184 and the Ollama sample redirects it into card.txt and then into the model prompt.\n\nImpact: Terminal display spoofing/hiding of the generated card on the victim's machine; untrusted control bytes forwarded into LLM prompts and tool messages. No code execution; integrity of what the user sees is the affected property.\n\nExploit scenario: A victim copies directions from a hostile web page or message into directions.txt and runs `python3 rewrite_directions.py ... < directions.txt`. A step with no lexicon hit, e.g. 'Walk to the bakery.\\x1b[2J\\x1b]0;...\\x07\\x1b[8m...', is printed unchanged; the terminal clears or hides text, rewrites the window title, or (on terminals supporting OSC 52) writes the clipboard. In the Ollama flow the same bytes are embedded in the prompt argv.\n\nPreconditions: victim feeds attacker-authored directions to the CLI or the Ollama/Codex pipelines; step contains no direction word (line 118) or no landmark/anchor (line 141) so it is passed through rather than replaced.\n\nRecommendation: Apply the same control-character stripping to each step before it is appended at lines 118, 138 and 141 (e.g. `step = re.sub(r\"[\\x00-\\x1f\\x7f]\", \"\", step)` after the split at 103, or run sanitize() without the length cap on step text), and document that output is sanitized for terminal use.\n\nCWE-150. Severity LOW, confidence high."
          },
