        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-697",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "sanitizeName denylist omits Unicode bidi/format controls, letting an OSM-editable name render a direction word that the residual-direction-word check cannot see.\n\nOSM `name` tags are editable by any registered OSM user and are the only untrusted text that reaches a route card through fetchLandmarks/parseOverpassResponse. sanitizeName strips ASCII controls and <>`{}[] but passes U+202A-U+202E, U+2066-U+2069 (bidi overrides/isolates), U+200B-U+200F and U+2028/U+2029 unchanged; a name such as `Cafe‮tfel nrut` is emitted verbatim into \"Turn toward ...\" and displays as \"turn left\" in any bidi-aware renderer, while findDirectionWords (which scans the logical string `tfel nrut`) reports no residual direction word.\n\nImpact: Integrity of the route card for a vulnerable user: a visually present direction instruction that the library guarantees it never emits and that its residual check does not report. No code execution or data exposure.\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker edits the OSM `name` of a feature within 120 m of a decision point on a route a DTD user is known to take, setting it to e.g. `Cafe‮tfel nrut dna ssorc` (RLO + reversed text). The victim's /landmark-route-script or /area-landmarks run fetches it via fetchLandmarks; sanitizeName leaves the RLO intact; the rewriter emits \"Turn toward Cafe‮tfel nrut dna ssorc.\" and reports residual direction words: none. Rendered in a chat UI or browser the line reads \"Turn toward Cafe cross and turn left\", a bare direction word in the card built for someone who cannot bind direction words, with the tool's own safety check reporting it clean. Requires the attacker to anticipate the victim's area and the edit to survive OSM moderation long enough to be fetched.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker can edit OSM data near a location the victim will query (any registered OSM account); The card is viewed in a renderer that honours Unicode bidi controls (browsers and most chat UIs do; many terminals do not); Edit must persist until the victim's fetch.\n\nRecommendation: Replace the denylist with an allowlist or add the Unicode control/format categories: strip `\\p{Cc}`, `\\p{Cf}` (covers bidi overrides, isolates, zero-width and BOM) and U+2028/U+2029 in both sanitizeName implementations, e.g. `name.replace(/[\\p{Cc}\\p{Cf}\u2028\u2029<>`{}\\[\\]]/gu, \"\")`. Apply the same change to lib/instruction-rewriter.ts:99 and to the inline `clean` in samples/n8n-landmark-lookup-workflow.json, and add a bidi test to the \"Untrusted OSM data\" suite.\n\nCWE-184. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "lib/overpass.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 112,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "  return name.replace(/[\\u0000-\\u001f\\u007f<>`{}\\[\\]]/g, \"\").trim().slice(0, 120);"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "sanitizeName",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "sanitizeName"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
