        {
          "ruleId": "uncategorized",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Audited document bytes are echoed to the terminal without stripping ESC/C0 control sequences.\n\nf.excerpt is produced by excerptOf() in lib/content-check.ts:106-109, which only collapses JS \\s (whitespace) and truncates to 140 chars; ESC (0x1B), BEL, and other C0 bytes present in the attacker-authored file pass straight to console.log. The same happens at line 74 for `rewrite`, where rewriteInstructions returns the raw step text (lib/instruction-rewriter.ts:141 and :180) for any step it cannot rewrite. The library deliberately strips \\u0000-\\u001f from landmark names, anchors and start-facing text (sanitizeName) precisely to prevent this class of injection, but the primary document content, the largest untrusted input, is not filtered.\n\nImpact: Integrity of what the operator sees on screen: findings (notably the prompt-injection warning the tool exists to surface) can be hidden or overwritten; on older or misconfigured terminals, OSC sequences may do more. No direct code execution or data access.\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker sends a DTD user a 'directions' file containing, for example, `Turn left \\x1b[8m.` followed by prompt-injection text. Running `wayfinding-audit check directions.txt` prints the direction-without-landmark finding whose excerpt includes SGR 8 (conceal); on terminals honouring it, every subsequent line, including the `[warning] prompt-injection-suspected` finding and the `Prompt injection suspected: yes` footer, is rendered invisible, so a human reviewer sees a report that appears to end cleanly. Other payloads can clear the screen, move the cursor to overwrite earlier findings, or set the window title via OSC. The process exit code is unaffected; the attack targets the human reading the output. The `rewrite` subcommand (line 74) echoes raw step text the same way. `--json` output is safe because JSON.stringify escapes control characters.\n\nPreconditions: Victim runs `check` or `rewrite` (without --json) on an attacker-supplied file; Output goes to an interactive terminal that honours the chosen escape sequences (SGR conceal, cursor movement, OSC); Attacker must place the sequence inside a sentence that produces a finding (check) or inside any step (rewrite).\n\nRecommendation: Strip C0/C1 control characters other than \\t from excerpts and steps before printing (e.g. apply the existing sanitizeName-style regex /[\\u0000-\\u0008\\u000b-\\u001f\\u007f-\\u009f]/g in excerptOf() and in audit.ts before each console.log), or route all human-readable output through one helper that does so. Add a test in tooling/test-security.ts asserting no byte in 0x00-0x1F/0x7F-0x9F survives in CheckReport.excerpt or RewriteResult.steps.\n\nCWE-150. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "tooling/cli-template/bin/audit.ts",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
